This is a brief overview of MK1, as well as some useful idioms.

MK1 supports:

- [ok]'s (and [MK2]'s) basic opcode set, only in lowercase, and now the number suffix for these opcodes is required (rather than a lack of a suffix inserting an implicit `3`), and the skip flag is denoted with a `?` prefix rather than a suffix.
- [MK2]'s hex literal instruction still uses `#`, only the skip flag is placed as a prefix like the other opcodes
- [MK2]'s comments
- [MK2]'s label declarations with `.` prefix and label "pushes" with `@`. Label "pushes" also use `?` as a prefix, like the other opcodes.
- [Uxntal]-style string syntax via a `$` prefix
- [Uxntal]-style raw hex sequences using a `^` prefix, allowing for inserting raw bytes into a program without them being pushed to the stack.

[ok]: https://github.com/htogta/ok
[MK2]: https://github.com/htogta/MK2
[Uxntal]: https://wiki.xxiivv.com/site/uxntal.html

MK1 also adds two new pseudo-instructions- `call` and `return`. These are handled in an initial translation phase before labels are handled in the assembly process (the phase that previously handled macros in [MK2]).

`call` expands into roughly the following:

```
@return-location psh3 jmp3 .return-location
```

Where `return-location` is replaced by a generated (and random/unique) label name each time `call` is used.

`return` expands into the following:

```
pop3 jmp3
```

It simply pops an address off of the return stack, and jumps to it.

MK1 also supports "anonymous labels" with square brackets, like so:

```
( this code implements "neq1", pushing ff is a!=b, otherwise 0, where a and b are 1 byte wide )
[
  cmp1 #01 and1 #ff xor1 #01 add1 return
]
```

Anonymous labels get converted like so:

```
@anon-end jmp3 .anon-start ( skip to the end and declare a unique lbl name )
  cmp1 #01 and1 #ff xor1 #01 add1 return
.anon-end
@anon-start ( the original label gets left on the stack )
```

Within an anonymous label, you can use the "start" and "end" keywords, which will push the address of the start of the current label block and the end of the label block, respectively. So this:

```
[ start end ]
```

Translates to this:

```
@anon-end jmp3 .anon-start ( [ )
  @anon-start ( "start" )
  @anon-end ( "end" )
.anon-end @anon-start ( ] )
```

You can use these to essentially create "quotations" (anonymous functions) that you can call:

```
[ #02 add1 return ] 
#03 call
```

They also make conditionals a little bit easier. Something like `if (condition) then (truebody)` could look something like this:

```
( condition ) 
[ ( truebody ) #00 ] 
?jmp3 drp3
```

Note that with this construction, not counting the size of the condition code or the bodies, a conditional adds 13 bytes of overhead.

The following works for a `if (condition) then (truebody) else (falsebody)` construction, and is good for when you have multiple bodies (like an if-elseif-else construction). You'd simply add the next condition (the "elseif" and its body) before the `.endif`:

```
( condition ) 
[ ( truebody ) @endif jmp3 ] 
?jmp3 drp3
( falsebody )
.endif
```

This construction adds 16 rather than 13 bytes of overhead, so if you know for certain that there's only an "if" and an "else" body (no "elseif"), I'd use the former construction rather than this one.

`if (cond1) then (body1) elseif (cond2) then (body2) else (body3)` looks like:

```
( cond1 ) 
[ ( body1 ) @endif jmp3 ] 
?jmp3 drp3
( cond2 )
[ (body2) @endif jmp3 ]
?jmp3 drp3
( body3 )
.endif
```

They're also helpful for looping constructs, and the `start` and `end` keywords allow for continue-like constructions. Something like `while (cond) (body)` (with a break and a continue, for demonstrative purposes) would look like this:

```
[ 
  ( cond ) #ff xor1 @endloop ?jmp3 drp3 
  ( body )
  start jmp3 ( this is a "continue ")
  @endloop jmp3 ( this is a "break" )
] jmp3
.endloop
```

Whereas for something like a `do (body) while (cond)` may look like this:

```
[
  ( body )
  ( cond ) #ff xor1 @endloop ?jmp3 drp3 
] jmp3
.endloop
```

And a `until (cond) do (body)` (basically a `while not (cond) do (body)`) is like this:

```
[ 
  ( cond ) @endloop ?jmp3 drp3 
  ( body )
] jmp3
.endloop
```

On top of all of that, you could even use anonymous labels to declare "variables" that are padded at the appropriate widths, and they're skipped immediately:

```
[ .bytevar nop1 .wordvar nop1 nop1 nop1 ] drp3
#ff @bytevar str1 ( store ff to bytevar )
#01abcd @wordvar str1 ( store 1abcd to wordvar )
@bytevar lod1 ( load ff from bytevar )
@wordvar lod3 ( load 1abcd from wordvar )
```
